ilibration  is not very favorable so this reaction  does take some time we can carry out  this reaction more efficiently using  sulfur trioxide in sulfuric acid this  reagent is called fuming sulfuric acid  and when you open the bottle it does  indeed smoke do this in a fume hood it's  very corrosive this mechanism can be  drawn a couple ways notice above we  generated a protonated form of sulfur  trioxide now the protonation of so3 may  be the first step in this reaction under  certain conditions however benzene can  also attack sulfur trioxide directly and  we'll show that mechanism here benzene  attacks so3 pushing the electrons up  onto oxygen restoring aromaticity in  this molecule is a strong driving force  so some people opt to show a spontaneous  deprotonation here but we also have  appropriate bases in solution to do this  deprotonation if we use sulfur trioxide  to do the deprotonation we can create  that great electrophile that we  generated in the above mechanism so  we'll show another molecule of sulfur  trioxide deprotonating benzene and a  final protonation of this anion will  give our sulphonic acid which was drawn  above let's take a look at the  properties of the sulphonic acid  derivative that we've just made this is  an electron withdrawing group we can  show how electron density is pulled out  of the Ring using one of the sulfur  oxygen double bonds this resonance form  has positive character at the ortho  position of the aromatic ring we can  continue to push electrons around the  ring and show that there's positive  character at the para and the other  ortho position of this aromatic ring  thus the sulphonic acid group is a  deactivating group  it pulls the electrons out of the ring  that benzene needs to undergo further  electrophilic aromatic substitution  reactions the electrons within the ring  are less available to attack  electrophiles on this compound the meta  carbons are the most electron rich so  when we treat benzene sulfonic acid w